     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Knitr does not seem to like it when I specify a lavaan model in my main .rnw file. The code chunk runs fine, but I can&#39;t get the document to compile. I get the error:</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Grande&#39;;font-size:12px;border-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:Monaco;outline:none;border-style:none;line-height:1.45;font-size:9pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\">Error in file(file, &quot;r&quot;) : cannot open the connection</pre></span></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div>So as described in this <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19469561/knitr-lavaan-and-subscript-out-of-bounds\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19469561/knitr-lavaan-and-subscript-out-of-bounds&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432514176000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1-ChwPqQcYXk2Y6K2emLrk\">SO post</a>, I tried defining a lavaan model in an external file and using readLines to grab the model.</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"margin-bottom:10px;padding:5px;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(238,238,238);font-family:Consolas,Menlo,Monaco,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,&#39;DejaVu Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Bitstream Vera Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace,serif;overflow:auto;width:auto;max-height:600px;word-wrap:normal;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18px\"><code style=\"font-family:Consolas,Menlo,Monaco,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,&#39;DejaVu Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Bitstream Vera Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace,serif\"><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">mod</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">.</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:rgb(128,0,0)\">1f</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">.</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:rgb(128,0,0)\">0</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\"> </span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\"> readLines</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">(</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:rgb(128,0,0)\">&quot;scripts/mod.lav.f1.0&quot;</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">)</span></code></pre></div><div><br></div><div>It runs fine when running R code chunks in RStudio, but knitting to a pdf with knitr throws a new error:</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Grande&#39;;font-size:12px;border-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"border-style:none;font-family:Monaco;outline:none;line-height:1.45;font-size:9pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\">Error in lines[[i]] : subscript out of bounds</pre></span></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Has anyone had any problems like this using knitr and lavaan? Is there a way in cfa() to call the model from an external file, like with specifyModel() in sem</div></div><div><pre style=\"margin-bottom:10px;padding:5px;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(238,238,238);font-family:Consolas,Menlo,Monaco,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,&#39;DejaVu Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Bitstream Vera Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace,serif;overflow:auto;width:auto;max-height:600px;word-wrap:normal;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18px\"><code style=\"font-family:Consolas,Menlo,Monaco,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,&#39;DejaVu Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Bitstream Vera Sans Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace,serif\"><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">model</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">.</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">mydata </span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\"> specifyModel</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">(</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">file </span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">=</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\"> </span><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:rgb(128,0,0)\">&#39;model.spec&#39;</span><span style=\"background-color:transparent\">)</span></code></pre></div><div>where model.spec contains the model specifications?</div></div>"
    ]
